#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tezgah-task - the active task: one plan's phase and its path allowlist.

  tezgah-task start <NNN|path> --phase P [--allow GLOB ... | --any-path]   activate a plan
  tezgah-task phase P [--any-path]                            change the active task's phase
  tezgah-task allow GLOB ... | --any-path --why REASON         replace the allowlist, recording why
  tezgah-task stop                                            deactivate (clear phase)
  tezgah-task status [--json]                                 the active task, then every open plan: progress, what it waits on, what can run at once
  tezgah-task review <NNN|path> <reviewer> approve|changes    record a fresh review's verdict
  tezgah-task close <NNN|path> done|discarded [--resolved TEXT] move a plan to .tezgah/plans/done, deactivate it, remove merged worktrees
  tezgah-task tick <NNN|path> <n> [--undo]                   tick (or untick) Acceptance item n, counted from 1
  tezgah-task note <NNN|path> state|next "<text>"            replace the body of `## State` (the newest one) or `## Next`
  tezgah-task slice open --id ID [--kind agent|job] [--host H] [--agent A] [--brief TEXT|--brief-file F] [--parent P] [--cwd PATH]
                                                              record a delegated slice (a re-open counts one more attempt)
  tezgah-task slice done ID | slice stop ID [--class C]      close a slice record
  tezgah-task slice reconcile [--prune]                       every open or stopped slice with its resume brief; --prune also removes landed worktrees and branches
  tezgah-task slice resume ID                                 the native resume path, else a re-dispatch brief; marks the record resumed
  tezgah-task slice stats [--window DAYS] [--json]            counts per status, failure class and host, and the RSI lever candidates
  tezgah-task slice board [--parent P]                        the open sibling slices: what each owns, its worktree, branch and last commit
  tezgah-task slice merge-plan [--base REF]                   the done slice branches in merge order, with their forecast conflicts

The record is the plan file itself: `phase:` activates it, `allowed_paths:` is
the scope a write has to stay inside, and at most one open plan carries a phase
because `start` clears the others. The orchestrating session runs every command
here itself as the work moves - the lifecycle is never handed back to the user -
and this CLI is the record's only writer: the gate refuses a write to the plan
file, because the checks below are made here and a retyped frontmatter would
skip them. `phase implementation` (or `verification`) is what lets a write
through, so the phase changes when the work reaches that point and not before.
`tick` and `note` are the record's body writers for the same reason: they write
an Acceptance box or the State/Next text on any open plan, active or not, keep
the frontmatter but `updated:`, and commit nothing.

The allowlist is the plan's own: plan-add writes `allowed_paths:` when it
creates the plan, and `start` without `--allow` keeps it. A writing phase with
no allowlist is refused unless `--any-path` says so explicitly, and the record
keeps where its scope came from (`allowed_from:`), which `status` prints.

The verification phase asks for one review of the plan's whole diff: `review`
records its verdict as
`review: <reviewer> <verdict> <date>` together with `reviewed_sha:` (the
project's HEAD it read) and `review_round:`, so the next round reads only
`git diff <reviewed_sha>..HEAD`, and refuses a third round: the budget is two
rounds per plan; `close NNN done` refuses a plan whose review is missing or not
`approve`, unless both rounds are spent and `--resolved` records the fix the
session made and the test that proves it. `close` on the active task clears its phase in the same write, then
removes each linked worktree whose branch has landed in HEAD, `main`, `master`
or the remote's default branch (an ancestor of it, or a squash whose merge
would change nothing) and which holds nothing else (no uncommitted or unknown
ignored file, no `.tezgah` data, a branch someone committed on, no Orca agent
still at work; Orca down keeps them all); every worktree it keeps is printed
with the reason.

Two things the plan owes are asked here, at the only moment they can still be
fixed - the phase move - because this CLI is the record's only writer and the
refusal is
this CLI's: a writing phase needs every Acceptance item to name the command
proving it (or say `unverifiable` and why), since the report CI runs afterwards
refuses an open plan on any one item that does neither, through the same
predicate (`tezgah_task.unproven`); and `implementation` needs the plan's spike
answered, so the build starts on evidence rather than on the guess the spike
exists to retire.

`status` lists every open plan after the active task: its Acceptance boxes
ticked, the plans its `after:` still waits on, and - on a `parallel:` line - the
ready plans whose `allowed_paths:` share no prefix, which can run at once. A
status outside open|blocked, or an `after:` id no plan carries, is a `FAIL` line.

Entering `implementation` also records where the risky work returns to: a
`checkpoint:` field holding the pre-work commit's sha, or `pending <sha>` while
the tree still holds changes no commit names - `<sha>` being the `HEAD` of that
moment. The gate refuses a write in the phase only while the recorded sha is
still HEAD and the tree is still dirty, so committing is the whole way out of it
and the commit clears the refusal by itself; the per-file snapshots
(bin/tezgah-rollback) are not one state to return to.

`slice` keeps the slice journal (hooks/tezgah_task.py): one record per delegated
slice under `.tezgah/slices/` of the main checkout, opened by the post-tool path
on a spawn call or by `slice open`, so a parent crash, an omp `/clear`, a quota
stop or a model or host switch loses no slice. `reconcile` reads each open omp
record's child transcript and closes the ones that ended, then prints one
resume brief per open or stopped slice; `--prune` first runs the same worktree
removal `close` does, deletes (`git branch -d`) every local branch that has
landed and is checked out nowhere - never main, master or the current branch -
and marks done each record whose branch landed with a commit of its own.
`resume` prints the host's native path first (omp `write agent://<id>` while
the parent session lives, Claude `SendMessage`), then a brief any host or model
can re-dispatch: the original brief plus the last commit, the branch and the
uncommitted files. `stats` flags a `class+host` signature at 3 events, or at 5%
of a window holding 20 or more slices, and names its lever from a closed set.
`board` lists the open slices one parent spawned - the session's own, or for a
subagent its parent's - with the `Owns:` globs of each brief (the lease the
gate holds siblings to), worktree, branch and last commit. `merge-plan` forecasts
each done slice branch against `--base` (HEAD) and against the others with `git
merge-tree`, leaves out a `merge=union` file such as CHANGELOG.md, and orders
them fewest conflicts first, a branch touching MANIFEST or the citation baseline
last. `review ... approve`
is refused while a record whose id or agent is that reviewer is not done.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 a refusal or a problem - one `FAIL <what>` line per problem,
with nothing written - 2 misuse.
"""
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(HERE, "hooks"))
import tezgah_task as tt  # noqa: E402


def repo_root(path):
    """The repository this run is about: git's own answer, else the directory the
    CLI was called in - a plan under a tree git does not know is still a plan."""
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", path, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True)
        if out.returncode == 0 and out.stdout.strip():
            return out.stdout.strip()
    except OSError:
        pass
    return os.path.realpath(path)


def misuse(message):
    """Misuse exits 2, so a caller can tell it from a record that refuses."""
    print("tezgah-task: %s" % message, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def today():
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")


def read(path):
    with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        return fh.read()


def open_dir(root):
    return os.path.join(root, ".tezgah", "plans", "open")


def plans(root):
    return sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(open_dir(root), "*.md")))


def plan_path(root, ref):
    """The plan file `ref` names: a three-digit id picks the single `NNN-*.md`
    under .tezgah/plans/open, anything else is a path. Returns (path, problem) with one
    of the two set."""
    if os.path.isfile(ref):
        return os.path.realpath(ref), None
    if re.fullmatch(r"\d{3}", ref):
        found = [p for p in plans(root) if os.path.basename(p).startswith(ref + "-")]
        if not found:
            return None, "no plan %s under %s" % (ref, open_dir(root))
        if len(found) > 1:
            return None, "%s matches %d plans: %s" % (ref, len(found), ", ".join(found))
        return found[0], None
    return None, "no such plan %r (a three-digit id, or a path)" % ref


def active_here(root):
    """The active task as every reader sees it - resolved by the module, so the
    CLI and the gate cannot disagree about which plan is active."""
    return tt.active(root, root)


def problems(items):
    for problem in items:
        print("FAIL %s" % problem)
    return 1


def write_fields(path, **fields):
    """Write the record, turning an I/O failure into the problem channel: a plan
    that cannot be written is a problem with the plan, not with how the command
    was called, and it must exit 1 with nothing half-applied."""
    try:
        tt.set_fields(path, **fields)
    except OSError as exc:
        return ["cannot write %s: %s" % (path, exc)]
    return []


ANY_PATH = "--any-path"
VERDICTS = ("approve", "changes")
# The review budget, enforced here and nowhere else: `review` refuses a third
# round, and every text that states the rule (the reviewer agent, the contract,
# plan-sync) says the same two rounds.
REVIEW_ROUNDS = 2
# Under this many changed lines one reviewer reads every dimension; at or above
# it the round fans out. ponytail: a line count, not a risk model - a small diff
# that touches a trust boundary is the brief's to route to security.
SMALL_DIFF = 300


def allow_source(text):
    """The allowlist and where it came from, as the phrase `status` prints: the
    plan's own `allowed_paths:` (written when the plan was created), the CLI's
    `--allow`/`allow` (`allowed_from:` names which), or an explicit
    `--any-path`. No list and no `--any-path` is what a writing phase refuses."""
    allow = tt.allowed_paths(text)
    fields = tt.frontmatter(text)
    source = fields.get("allowed_from") or ""
    why = (" (why: %s)" % fields["allowed_why"]) if fields.get("allowed_why") else ""
    if allow:
        return allow, (("from tezgah-task %s" % source) + why) if source else "from the plan"
    if source == ANY_PATH:
        return allow, "any path in the repo (--any-path)" + why
    return allow, "any path in the repo (no allowlist)"


def checkpoint_state(value, path):
    """The checkpoint line `show` prints for a plan in `implementation`: the sha
    the work returns to, or the commit the phase waits for - named with the sha it
    has to move off and with the one command that does it, because that command's
    completion is what clears the gate's refusal.

    The gate's refusal is `HEAD == the recorded sha AND the tree is dirty`, so
    the line reads the checkout rather than the record alone: after the commit
    the refusal names - or after the user discards that uncommitted work - the
    record still carries `pending <sha>` until the next phase move rewrites it,
    and a status that kept printing "writes are refused" would report a boundary
    the gate has already lifted. The predicate is spelled the same way as
    `tezgah_gate.checkpoint_reason`'s, so the two cannot disagree about which
    state the phase is in."""
    sha = tt.checkpoint_sha(value)
    if sha:
        root = repo_root(os.path.dirname(path) or ".")
        head = git_out(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
        listed = git_out(root, "status", "--porcelain")
        if not head or head != sha or not listed:
            print("  checkpoint: satisfied - %s, so this phase's writes are "
                  "allowed" % ("HEAD moved off %s" % sha if head != sha
                               else "the tree is clean again"))
        else:
            print("  checkpoint: pending on %s - the tree held uncommitted changes "
                  "when the phase began, so this phase's writes are refused until "
                  "the commit that moves HEAD off it: %s"
                  % (sha, tt.checkpoint_command(path)))
    elif value:
        print("  checkpoint: %s" % value)
    else:
        print("  checkpoint: none (one is recorded when the phase moves to "
              "implementation)")


def show(path):
    """Print the record as it stands on disk, read back through the parser the
    gate uses: the CLI reports what was written, not what it meant to write."""
    text = read(path)
    fields = tt.frontmatter(text)
    allow, source = allow_source(text)
    phase = fields.get("phase") or ""
    print("task %s: phase %s" % (fields.get("id") or os.path.basename(path).split("-")[0],
                                 phase or "(none)"))
    print("  allowed: %s" % ("%s (%s)" % (", ".join(allow), source) if allow else source))
    print("  path: %s" % path)
    if phase == "implementation":
        checkpoint_state(fields.get("checkpoint"), path)
    if phase == "verification":
        verification_steps(text, path)
    return 0


def diff_base(root):
    """The commit the plan's work branched from: the merge-base with the first
    of main/master/origin/main that exists, else a placeholder."""
    for ref in ("main", "master", "origin/main"):
        out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "merge-base", "HEAD", ref],
                             capture_output=True, text=True)
        if out.returncode == 0 and out.stdout.strip():
            return out.stdout.strip()[:12]
    return "<base>"


def git_out(root, *args):
    """One git command's stdout in `root`, or None when git cannot answer: not a
    repository, no such revision, git missing. Every caller below reads None as
    "this question has no answer here" and refuses nothing on the strength of it,
    so a tree git cannot describe is never held to a rule about its state."""
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root] + list(args),
                             capture_output=True, text=True)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return out.stdout.strip() if out.returncode == 0 else None


def tree_dirty(root):
    """Does the project's working tree hold changes no commit names? `git status
    --porcelain` is the whole answer, and a tree git cannot describe reads as
    clean - a refusal has to rest on a state the user can see, and a checkout
    without git is not the tree this rule is about."""
    listed = git_out(root, "status", "--porcelain")
    return bool(listed)


def checkpoint_fields(root, phase):
    """The `checkpoint:` the record gets from a move to `phase`: the pre-work
    commit's sha, or `pending <sha>` while the tree holds uncommitted work -
    `<sha>` being the `HEAD` of that moment, which the commit the gate's refusal
    names moves off. That comparison is what lets the commit clear the refusal by
    itself, instead of the refusal outliving it and locking the phase for an agent
    that may not rerun this CLI.

    `{}` for every phase but `implementation` - the boundary is the writing
    phase's own, and `discovery` writes nothing to return from. A tree with
    nothing committed at all (a fresh `git init`) has no sha to name, so it takes
    a bare `pending`: nothing to compare against, and the gate then holds nothing
    to it."""
    if phase != "implementation":
        return {}
    sha = git_out(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    if tree_dirty(root):
        return {"checkpoint": ("%s %s" % (tt.CHECKPOINT_PENDING, sha)) if sha
                else tt.CHECKPOINT_PENDING}
    return {"checkpoint": sha or tt.CHECKPOINT_PENDING}


def phase_problems(phase, text, path):
    """Every reason this plan cannot enter `phase`, as problem-channel lines: an
    Acceptance section in which nothing can check the work, and - for
    `implementation` - a spike that has not answered yet.

    Both are read from the plan this CLI is about to move, through the same
    readers the CI report uses, so the phase the gate then holds a write to is a
    phase the plan can actually be checked in. They live here, at the phase move,
    and not in the gate: the phase has one writer, this command - a gate rule
    would have to re-read the same body on every write to ask a question whose
    answer changes only here."""
    out = []
    if phase in tt.WRITE_PHASES:
        items = tt.acceptance_gap(text)
        if items:
            out.append(
                "%s cannot enter %s: %d of its %d Acceptance items name no "
                "command and declare no `unverifiable` (read by "
                "hooks/tezgah_task.py:acceptance_items; at %s), so that work has "
                "no check to be held to. Give each one the command that proves "
                "it, or `unverifiable` and why; "
                "`tezgah-render-table --acceptance --strict` refuses the same items"
                % (path, phase, len(items), len(tt.acceptance_items(text)),
                   ", ".join("%s:%d" % (path, i["line"]) for i in items)))
    if phase == "implementation" and tt.spike_unanswered(text):
        out.append(
            "%s cannot enter implementation: its spike is unanswered - `spike:` "
            "names the question and `spike_recorded:` names no answer, so the "
            "build would start on the guess the spike exists to retire. Record "
            "where the answer landed (`spike_recorded:`) first" % path)
    return out


def changed_lines(root, base):
    """Lines `git diff <base>..HEAD` adds and removes, or None when git cannot
    say (no base, not a repository)."""
    stat = git_out(root, "diff", "--shortstat", base + "..HEAD") \
        if base != "<base>" else None
    if stat is None:
        return None
    return sum(int(n) for n in re.findall(r"(\d+) (?:insertion|deletion)", stat))


# The checks deferred out of the turn to the merge step (AGENTS.md "Checks, by
# tier"): (the file that marks the repo has it, the command), in run order.
MERGE_CHECKS = (
    ("tests/impacted.py", "python3 tests/impacted.py --all"),
    ("tests/neuter_matrix.py", "TEZGAH_E2E_STRICT=1 python3 tests/neuter_matrix.py"),
    ("bin/tezgah-docs", "python3 bin/tezgah-docs --citations"),
    ("bin/tezgah-docs", "python3 bin/tezgah-docs --clarity"),
    ("skills/plan-add/render_table.py",
     "python3 skills/plan-add/render_table.py --acceptance --strict"),
)


def verification_steps(text, path):
    """What the verification phase requires before the plan is done: the plan's
    own acceptance commands run once on the final tree, unpiped, and ONE review
    of the plan's whole diff, recorded with `review` - `close NNN done` refuses
    without an approve. Round one fans out one reviewer per dimension, round two
    reads only the delta since the sha round one recorded, and REVIEW_ROUNDS is
    the budget `review` enforces."""
    fields = tt.frontmatter(text)
    ident = fields.get("id") or os.path.basename(path).split("-")[0]
    # every item, not only the ones the command reader recognises: a bare
    # `pytest` is a command it does not read as one, and must not vanish here
    items = ["%s%s" % (i["text"], "" if i["state"] == "checkable" else
                       "  [%s]" % ("unverifiable" if i["state"] == "unverifiable"
                                   else "names no command"))
             for i in tt.acceptance_items(text)]
    # the project, not the private repository `.tezgah` may hold
    root = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(path), "..", "..", ".."))
    reviewed = fields.get("reviewed_sha") or "(no commit)"
    prior = fields.get("review_round") or ""
    rounds = int(prior) if prior.isdigit() else 0
    print("verification - required before this plan is done:")
    print("  1. prove the acceptance items ONCE, on the final tree - not again per "
          "review round - every command run unpiped "
          "(`cmd > /tmp/check.log 2>&1`, then read the file); items that share "
          "no state go out as tool calls in ONE message, one suite run at a time:")
    for item in items or ["(the plan has no Acceptance items)"]:
        print("     - %s" % item)
    # the deferred tier: never inside a turn, run once here at the merge step
    deferred = [cmd for rel, cmd in MERGE_CHECKS
                if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(root, rel))]
    if deferred:
        print("     the merge step's one run of the deferred checks: %s"
              % ", ".join("`%s`" % c for c in deferred))
    if rounds and review_verdict(text) == "approve":
        print("  2. the review approved in round %d at %s: no further round"
              % (rounds, reviewed))
        print("  3. `tezgah-task close %s done` once the work lands" % ident)
        return
    if rounds >= REVIEW_ROUNDS:
        print("  2. the %d-round budget is spent: `tezgah-task review` refuses a "
              "third round. Fix a critical or major finding still confirmed "
              "yourself, prove it with a test and close with `--resolved`; fix a "
              "minor or suggestion one now, or record it fix-later under the "
              "plan's ## State only when it waits on a user decision (KARAR)"
              % REVIEW_ROUNDS)
        return
    if rounds:
        print("  2. review round 2 (the last): each confirmed critical or major "
              "finding is fixed with a regression test that failed first; one "
              "fresh tezgah-reviewer reads only the delta `git diff %s..HEAD`, the "
              "round-1 hunks and direct callers of the symbols it touches, and the "
              "test output above, and marks every round-1 finding closed or open; "
              "it does not re-run the suite" % reviewed)
    else:
        base = diff_base(root)
        size = changed_lines(root, base)
        print("  2. one review of the whole plan, at most %d rounds: spawn fresh "
              "tezgah-reviewer subagents in ONE message, each owning one dimension "
              "- correctness and contract, security, tests and performance - or one "
              "file group of a large diff, over `git diff %s..HEAD` and the test "
              "output above, then merge their findings into one verdict. Under %d "
              "changed lines one reviewer reads every dimension (this diff: %s), "
              "unless the diff touches auth, secrets or a gate, which keeps a "
              "security reviewer" % (REVIEW_ROUNDS, base, SMALL_DIFF,
                                     "unknown" if size is None else size))
        print("     only a confirmed critical or major finding is `changes` and "
              "opens round 2; a minor or suggestion one is fixed in the session "
              "without a new round, or recorded fix-later under ## State only "
              "when it waits on a user decision (KARAR), and the verdict is "
              "`approve`")
    print("  3. record its verdict: `tezgah-task review %s <reviewer> approve|changes`"
          " (records the reviewed sha);"
          " `tezgah-task close %s done` refuses without an approve" % (ident, ident))


def bad_phase(phase):
    return "phase %r is not one of %s" % (phase, ", ".join(tt.TASK_PHASES))


def scope_problem(phase, allow, any_path):
    """A writing phase with nothing to hold its writes to is refused unless the
    user said `--any-path`: an empty allowlist reads as "anywhere", and a plan
    that forgot its scope must not grant that by omission."""
    if phase in tt.WRITE_PHASES and not allow and not any_path:
        return ["phase %s needs an allowlist: the plan has no `allowed_paths:` and "
                "no --allow was given. Pass --allow GLOB ..., or --any-path to let "
                "the task write anywhere in the repo" % phase]
    return []


def cmd_start(args):
    if not args:
        return misuse("start needs a plan id or path")
    root = repo_root(os.getcwd())
    path, problem = open_plan(args[0])
    phase = None
    allow = []
    any_path = False
    rest = args[1:]
    i = 0
    while i < len(rest):
        if rest[i] == "--phase":
            if i + 1 >= len(rest):
                return misuse("--phase needs one of %s" % ", ".join(tt.TASK_PHASES))
            phase = rest[i + 1]
            i += 2
            continue
        if rest[i] == ANY_PATH:
            any_path = True
            i += 1
            continue
        if rest[i] == "--allow":
            i += 1
            first = i
            while i < len(rest) and not rest[i].startswith("--"):
                i += 1
            if i == first:
                return misuse("--allow needs at least one glob (--any-path for any path)")
            allow += rest[first:i]
            continue
        return misuse("unknown argument %r" % rest[i])
    if phase is None:
        return misuse("start needs --phase %s" % "|".join(tt.TASK_PHASES))
    if allow and any_path:
        return misuse("--allow and --any-path contradict each other")
    if phase not in tt.TASK_PHASES:
        return problems([bad_phase(phase)])
    if problem:
        return problems([problem])
    text = read(path)
    fields = {"phase": phase, "updated": today()}
    if allow or any_path:
        fields.update(allowed_paths=allow, allowed_from="--allow" if allow else ANY_PATH)
        if tt.frontmatter(text).get("allowed_why"):
            fields["allowed_why"] = ""  # the reason belonged to the scope replaced
    else:
        # the plan's own scope, as plan-add wrote it (or an earlier --allow)
        allow = tt.allowed_paths(text)
        any_path = tt.frontmatter(text).get("allowed_from") == ANY_PATH
    bad = (scope_problem(phase, allow, any_path)
           or phase_problems(phase, text, path))
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    fields.update(checkpoint_fields(root, phase))
    for other in plans(root):
        if other == path:
            continue
        if tt.frontmatter(read(other)).get("phase"):
            # one active task at a time: the plan that had the phase loses it
            # here rather than both being active and the gate reading whichever
            # sorts first
            bad = write_fields(other, phase="", updated=today())
            if bad:
                return problems(bad)
    bad = write_fields(path, **fields)
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    return show(path)


def cmd_phase(args):
    any_path = ANY_PATH in args
    args = [a for a in args if a != ANY_PATH]
    if len(args) != 1:
        return misuse("phase takes one of %s [--any-path]" % ", ".join(tt.TASK_PHASES))
    phase = args[0]
    if phase not in tt.TASK_PHASES:
        return problems([bad_phase(phase)])
    root = repo_root(os.getcwd())
    task = active_here(root)
    if not task:
        return problems(["no active task: start one with "
                         "`tezgah-task start <id> --phase %s`" % phase])
    text = read(task["path"])
    fields = {"phase": phase, "updated": today()}
    if any_path:
        fields.update(allowed_paths=[], allowed_from=ANY_PATH)
        if tt.frontmatter(text).get("allowed_why"):
            fields["allowed_why"] = ""
    granted = (any_path or tt.frontmatter(text).get("allowed_from")
               == ANY_PATH)
    bad = (scope_problem(phase, task["allowed_paths"], granted)
           or phase_problems(phase, text, task["path"]))
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    fields.update(checkpoint_fields(root, phase))
    bad = write_fields(task["path"], **fields)
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    return show(task["path"])


def cmd_allow(args):
    # the scope is the session's to widen, so the reason is the price: a widening
    # nobody can read back later is the unbounded task this record exists to stop
    why = ""
    if "--why" in args:
        i = args.index("--why")
        why = " ".join((args[i + 1:i + 2] or [""])[0].split())
        args = args[:i] + args[i + 2:]
    any_path = ANY_PATH in args
    args = [a for a in args if a != ANY_PATH]
    for arg in args:
        if arg.startswith("--"):
            return misuse("allow takes globs, not %r" % arg)
    if bool(args) == any_path:
        return misuse("allow takes globs, or --any-path alone to lift the scope")
    if not why:
        return misuse("allow takes --why \"<what in the plan reaches these paths>\"")
    task = active_here(repo_root(os.getcwd()))
    if not task:
        return problems(["no active task to widen: start one with "
                         "`tezgah-task start <id> --phase P --allow GLOB`"])
    # --any-path replaces the list with an empty one, which reads as "any path in
    # the repo" - and the gate skips the scope check entirely then, so this is
    # the way back out of a scope that turned out to be too narrow. A flag and
    # not an empty call, so lifting the scope is always said out loud.
    bad = write_fields(task["path"], allowed_paths=args, updated=today(),
                       allowed_from=ANY_PATH if any_path else "allow",
                       allowed_why=why)
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    return show(task["path"])


def cmd_stop(args):
    if args:
        return misuse("stop takes no arguments")
    task = active_here(repo_root(os.getcwd()))
    if not task:
        return problems(["no active task to stop"])
    bad = write_fields(task["path"], phase="", updated=today())
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    print("task %s stopped" % task["id"])
    return show(task["path"])


def open_plan(ref):
    """(path, problem) for a plan under `.tezgah/plans/open/`: `tick` and `note`
    write past the gate, so they take no other file (review of plan 083, F4:
    `note README.md next ...` rewrote any markdown file with a Next section)."""
    root = repo_root(os.getcwd())
    path, problem = plan_path(root, ref)
    if problem:
        return None, problem
    if os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(path)) != os.path.realpath(open_dir(root)):
        return None, "%s is not an open plan under %s" % (path, open_dir(root))
    return path, None


def cmd_tick(args):
    """Tick (or `--undo` untick) one Acceptance box: the plan's progress is the
    session's to record, and the gate refuses a direct write to the record."""
    undo = "--undo" in args
    args = [a for a in args if a != "--undo"]
    if len(args) != 2 or not args[1].isdigit():
        return misuse("tick takes <NNN|path> <n> [--undo]")
    path, problem = open_plan(args[0])
    if problem:
        return problems([problem])
    items = tt.acceptance_items(read(path))
    n = int(args[1])
    if not 1 <= n <= len(items):
        return problems(["%s has %d Acceptance items, not an item %d"
                         % (path, len(items), n)])
    item = items[n - 1]
    try:
        tt.set_box(path, item["line"], not undo)
    except OSError as exc:
        return problems(["cannot write %s: %s" % (path, exc)])
    bad = write_fields(path, updated=today())
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    print("- [%s] %s" % (" " if undo else "x", item["text"]))
    return 0


NOTE_SECTIONS = {"state": "State", "next": "Next"}


def cmd_note(args):
    """Replace the plan's `## State` (the newest one) or `## Next` body."""
    if len(args) != 3 or args[1] not in NOTE_SECTIONS or not args[2].strip():
        return misuse('note takes <NNN|path> state|next "<text>"')
    path, problem = open_plan(args[0])
    if problem:
        return problems([problem])
    heading = NOTE_SECTIONS[args[1]]
    try:
        found = tt.set_section(path, heading, args[2])
    except OSError as exc:
        return problems(["cannot write %s: %s" % (path, exc)])
    if not found:
        return problems(["%s has no `## %s` section" % (path, heading)])
    bad = write_fields(path, updated=today())
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    print("%s: ## %s updated" % (path, heading))
    return 0


def review_verdict(text):
    """The verdict of the plan's `review: <reviewer> <verdict> <date>` line, or
    None when it has none or names a verdict this CLI does not write."""
    parts = (tt.frontmatter(text).get("review") or "").split()
    return parts[1] if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[1] in VERDICTS else None


def budget_spent(path, ident):
    """The refusal once REVIEW_ROUNDS rounds are recorded: never a third round,
    and never a hand-off - the session settles what is left itself."""
    return ("%s has spent its %d-round review budget: never a third round. Fix a "
            "critical or major finding still confirmed yourself and prove it with "
            "a test, then `tezgah-task close %s done --resolved \"<the fix and the "
            "test that proves it>\"`; fix a minor or suggestion one now, or record "
            "it fix-later under the plan's ## State only when it waits on a "
            "user decision (KARAR); `close %s discarded` needs no review"
            % (path, REVIEW_ROUNDS, ident, ident))


def unfinished_review(root, reviewer, plan="", since=0):
    """The refusal of an `approve` while a slice journal record whose id or agent
    is `reviewer` is open, stopped or stale: a review slice that died on a
    provider limit had its approve recorded anyway, and a NameError it would have
    caught reached main (plan 078). A stale review is one whose end nobody saw,
    which reads no more than a stopped one. The omp records are reconciled with
    their transcripts first (`tt.pending`), so a review that finished reads as
    done. A record that names the plan active when it opened counts only for
    that `plan`; one that names none counts when it changed at or after `since`
    (epoch seconds; the plan's checkpoint commit). A `resumed` record has handed
    its work to the re-dispatch, whose own record is the one read. A record
    spawned as `reviewer` under its own id (`Reviewer-<digest6>`,
    `tt.open_slice`) counts too. Under TEZGAH_SESSION only this session's
    records and unowned ones count, by id, agent or name alike: another
    session's review is not this approve's. The refusal names the exact id,
    which `slice resume` and `slice done` take from any session."""
    from tezgah_paths import off
    if off("slices-off"):
        return []
    tt.pending(root)
    session = os.environ.get("TEZGAH_SESSION")

    def named(rec):
        return reviewer in (rec.get("id"), rec.get("agent"), rec.get("name")) and (
            not session or rec.get("parent") in (session, "", None))

    def ours(rec):
        if rec.get("plan"):
            return rec["plan"] == plan
        return (_stamp(rec.get("updated")) or 0) >= since

    return ["review slice %s is %s%s: let it finish, or resume it with `tezgah-task "
            "slice resume %s` and wait for that run; if the review was finished "
            "another way, record it with `tezgah-task slice done %s` - an approve "
            "from a review that did not finish reads nothing"
            % (rec["id"], rec.get("status"),
               (" (%s)" % rec["failure"]) if rec.get("failure") else "",
               rec["id"], rec["id"])
            for rec in tt.slices(root)
            if rec.get("status") in ("open", "stopped", "stale")
            and named(rec) and ours(rec)]


def cmd_review(args):
    if len(args) != 3 or args[2] not in VERDICTS:
        return misuse("review takes <NNN|path> <reviewer> %s" % "|".join(VERDICTS))
    path, problem = open_plan(args[0])
    if problem:
        return problems([problem])
    fields = tt.frontmatter(read(path))
    prior = fields.get("review_round") or ""
    if prior.isdigit() and int(prior) >= REVIEW_ROUNDS:
        return problems([budget_spent(path, os.path.basename(path).split("-")[0])])
    # the project's HEAD, never the private `.tezgah` repository's: the next
    # round's delta is `git diff <this>..HEAD` over the project
    root = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(path), "..", "..", ".."))
    if args[2] == "approve":
        # the plan's checkpoint commit (a sha, or `pending <sha>`) dates the work
        # this review is about; with none, every record counts
        mark = (fields.get("checkpoint") or "").split()
        since = (git_out(root, "log", "-1", "--format=%ct", mark[-1])
                 if mark and mark[-1] != tt.CHECKPOINT_PENDING else None)
        ident = fields.get("id") or os.path.basename(path).split("-")[0]
        bad = unfinished_review(root, args[1], ident,
                                int(since) if (since or "").isdigit() else 0)
        if bad:
            return problems(bad)
    sha = (git_out(root, "rev-parse", "HEAD") or "")[:12]
    record = {"review": "%s %s %s" % (args[1], args[2], today()),
              "review_round": str(int(prior) + 1 if prior.isdigit() else 1),
              "updated": today()}
    if sha:
        record["reviewed_sha"] = sha
    bad = write_fields(path, **record)
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    print("review recorded: %s %s round %s at %s (%s)" % (
        args[1], args[2], record["review_round"], sha or "(no commit)", path))
    return 0


def cmd_close(args):
    resolved = ""
    if "--resolved" in args:
        i = args.index("--resolved")
        resolved = " ".join((args[i + 1:i + 2] or [""])[0].split())
        args = args[:i] + args[i + 2:]
        if not resolved:
            return misuse("--resolved takes the fix and the test that proves it")
    if len(args) != 2 or args[1] not in ("done", "discarded"):
        return misuse("close takes <NNN|path> done|discarded [--resolved TEXT]")
    root = repo_root(os.getcwd())
    path, problem = plan_path(root, args[0])
    if problem:
        return problems([problem])
    if os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(path)) != os.path.realpath(open_dir(root)):
        return problems(["%s is not under %s" % (path, open_dir(root))])
    text = read(path)
    ident = os.path.basename(path).split("-")[0]
    # closing the active task deactivates it in the same write: the plan leaves
    # open/ anyway, and a separate `stop` first was one more step for nobody
    fields = {"status": args[1], "updated": today()}
    if tt.frontmatter(text).get("phase"):
        fields["phase"] = ""
    if args[1] == "done" and review_verdict(text) != "approve":
        prior = tt.frontmatter(text).get("review_round") or ""
        spent = prior.isdigit() and int(prior) >= REVIEW_ROUNDS
        if spent and resolved:
            # the budget is spent and the session fixed what was left: the fix
            # and its proof go on the record a later reader rejects it from
            fields["resolved"] = "%s %s" % (resolved, today())
        elif spent:
            return problems([budget_spent(path, ident)])
        else:
            return problems(["%s records no approving review: a fresh reviewer reads "
                             "the diff, then `tezgah-task review %s <reviewer> approve`"
                             % (path, ident)])
    target = os.path.join(root, ".tezgah", "plans", "done", os.path.basename(path))
    bad = write_fields(path, **fields)
    if bad:
        return problems(bad)
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(target), exist_ok=True)
        os.replace(path, target)
    except OSError as exc:
        return problems(["cannot move %s: %s" % (path, exc)])
    print("moved %s -> %s" % (path, target))
    for line in prune_worktrees(root):
        print(line)
    return 0


# Ignored output a slice regenerates on its own. Any other ignored path - an
# `.env`, a local database, a dataset - may be the only copy, so it keeps the
# worktree; a list rather than `--ignored` alone because every checkout that ran
# a test holds a cache.
REGENERABLE = frozenset(("__pycache__", ".pytest_cache", ".ruff_cache",
                         ".mypy_cache", "node_modules", ".venv", "venv",
                         ".DS_Store"))


def _landed(root, branch, base):
    """True when `branch`'s work is in `base`: an ancestor of it, or - for a
    squash or rebase merge, which leaves no ancestry - a branch whose merge into
    `base` would change nothing. `merge-tree --write-tree` needs git 2.38; an
    older git answers None, which reads as not landed and keeps the worktree."""
    if git_out(root, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", branch, base) is not None:
        return True
    # a branch whose commits cancel out (X then revert X) merges to base's tree
    # too, while its commits are in base nowhere: it needs a net change to count
    fork = git_out(root, "merge-base", base, branch)
    if not fork or git_out(root, "diff", "--quiet", fork, branch) is not None:
        return False
    merged = (git_out(root, "merge-tree", "--write-tree", base, branch) or "").split("\n")[0]
    return bool(merged) and merged == git_out(root, "rev-parse", base + "^{tree}")


def _keeps(root, path, branch, bases, agents):
    """Why the linked worktree `path` must stay, or None when its work is all in
    `bases` and it holds nothing else. Every question git cannot answer keeps it."""
    if not branch:
        return "detached HEAD"
    # -unormal: a `status.showUntrackedFiles=no` config would hide both lists
    if git_out(path, "status", "--porcelain", "-unormal") != "":
        return "uncommitted changes"
    for line in (git_out(path, "status", "--porcelain", "-unormal", "--ignored") or "").splitlines():
        rel = line[3:].strip('"').rstrip("/")
        if rel != ".tezgah" and not REGENERABLE & set(rel.split("/")):
            return "ignored file %s may be the only copy" % rel
    ws = os.path.join(path, ".tezgah")
    if os.path.isdir(ws):
        if set(os.listdir(ws)) - {".git"}:
            return "its .tezgah holds data"
        if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(ws, ".git")) and git_out(ws, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "HEAD"):
            return "its .tezgah has commits"
    if not any(_landed(root, branch, b) for b in bases):
        return "%s is not merged into %s" % (branch, " or ".join(bases))
    log = git_out(root, "reflog", "show", "--format=%gs", "refs/heads/" + branch) or ""
    if not any(entry.startswith(("commit:", "commit (amend):", "commit (initial):"))
               for entry in log.splitlines()):
        # a branch nobody committed on is a slice that has not started - even
        # one fast-forwarded to main, or one that only cherry-picked or merged
        # main in - not one whose work landed
        return "%s has no commit of its own yet" % branch
    busy = [a.get("state") for a in agents if a.get("state") != "done"]
    if busy:
        return "an agent there is %s" % busy[0]
    return None


def landing_bases(root):
    """The refs a branch's work counts as landed in, those that exist: HEAD,
    main, master, and the remote's default branch too - a PR merged on the host
    lands there first."""
    return [b for b in ("HEAD", "main", "master", "origin/HEAD", "origin/main",
                        "origin/master")
            if git_out(root, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", b)]


def prune_worktrees(root):
    """Remove every linked worktree of the repository whose branch has landed and
    which holds nothing else, so a closed plan leaves no checkout behind; one line
    per linked worktree, removed or kept with the reason. The main checkout and
    the one `close` runs in always stay. Under Orca the removal is Orca's (its
    card, terminals and merged branch go with it), else `git worktree remove`
    and a safe `git branch -d`. When Orca is installed but does not answer, its
    agents cannot be checked, so every worktree stays."""
    import tezgah_orca
    from tezgah_paths import worktrees
    found = worktrees(root)
    here = os.path.realpath(root)
    linked = [p for p in found[1:] if p != here]
    if not linked:
        return []
    ps = tezgah_orca.run(["worktree", "ps", "--limit", "1000"])
    rows = ps.get("worktrees") if isinstance(ps, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(rows, list) and tezgah_orca.cli():
        # down, or an answer in a shape this reader does not know: either way
        # its agents cannot be checked
        return ["worktree kept: %s (Orca did not answer, so its agents cannot be "
                "checked; run close again once `orca worktree ps` answers)" % p
                for p in linked]
    orca = {os.path.realpath(r.get("path") or ""): r for r in rows or []
            if isinstance(r, dict)}
    bases = landing_bases(root)
    out = []
    for path in linked:
        branch = git_out(path, "symbolic-ref", "-q", "--short", "HEAD")
        try:
            why = _keeps(root, path, branch, bases,
                         orca.get(path, {}).get("agents") or [])
        except OSError as exc:
            why = "cannot read it: %s" % exc
        if why is None:
            if path in orca:
                gone = tezgah_orca.run(["worktree", "rm", "--worktree", "path:" + path],
                                       timeout=60) is not None
                how = "orca worktree rm --worktree path:%s" % path
            else:
                gone = git_out(root, "worktree", "remove", path) is not None
                how = "git worktree remove %s" % path
                if gone:
                    git_out(root, "branch", "-d", branch)
            why = None if gone else "`%s` failed; run it and report its error" % how
        out.append("worktree %s: %s (%s)" % ("kept" if why else "removed", path,
                                             why or branch))
    return out


def cmd_status(args):
    as_json = "--json" in args
    rest = [a for a in args if a != "--json"]
    if rest:
        return misuse("status takes --json and nothing else (%r)" % rest[0])
    root = repo_root(os.getcwd())
    task = active_here(root)
    bad = []
    for path in plans(root):
        fields = tt.frontmatter(read(path))
        ident = fields.get("id") or os.path.basename(path).split("-")[0]
        phase = fields.get("phase") or ""
        if phase and phase not in tt.TASK_PHASES:
            bad.append({"id": ident, "phase": phase, "path": path,
                        "problem": "phase %r is not one of %s, so the plan is ignored (%s)"
                        % (phase, ", ".join(tt.TASK_PHASES), path)})
        status = fields.get("status") or ""
        if status not in tt.OPEN_STATUSES:
            bad.append({"id": ident, "path": path,
                        "problem": "status %r is not one of %s, which is what a plan "
                        "under .tezgah/plans/open carries (%s)"
                        % (status, ", ".join(tt.OPEN_STATUSES), path)})
    rows = tt.queue(root)
    for row in rows:
        for ident, state in row["after"]:
            if state == "missing":
                bad.append({"id": row["id"], "path": row["path"],
                            "problem": "`after: %s` names no plan under .tezgah/plans "
                            "(%s)" % (ident, row["path"])})
    together = tt.parallel(rows)
    if task:
        task["allowed_from"] = allow_source(read(task["path"]))[1]
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps({"active": task, "malformed": bad, "open": rows,
                          "parallel": together}, indent=2))
    else:
        if task:
            show(task["path"])
        else:
            print("no active task")
        if rows:
            print("open plans: %d, %d ready" % (len(rows), sum(r["ready"] for r in rows)))
        for row in rows:
            waits = ["%s (%s)" % (ident, state) for ident, state in row["after"]
                     if state != "done"]
            print("  %s %s, acceptance %d/%d checked, %s" % (
                row["id"], row["status"] or "(no status)", row["checked"], row["items"],
                "ready" if row["ready"] else
                "waits on %s" % ", ".join(waits) if waits else row["status"]))
        if together:
            print("parallel: %s - ready, and no two share an allowed_paths prefix"
                  % ", ".join(together))
        for row in bad:
            print("FAIL %s: %s" % (row["id"], row["problem"]))
    return 1 if bad else 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ slice

# The closed lever set a stats candidate is named from (the research line's
# RSI loop): one file or mechanism per failure class, never prose.
LEVERS = {"rate_limit": "hooks/tezgah_models.py fallback chain",
          "quota": "hooks/tezgah_models.py fallback chain",
          "cut_off": "per-slice deadline", "timeout": "per-slice deadline",
          "aborted": "wip: commit cadence", "duplicate": "journal background jobs",
          "error": "none (read the transcript)"}
STATS_WINDOW = 14
# A signature is a candidate at this many events, or at this share of the slices
# in the window once the window holds STATS_SHARE_FLOOR slices: below that, one
# event is already 5% and every stop would read as a pattern.
STATS_EVENTS = 3
STATS_SHARE = 0.05
STATS_SHARE_FLOOR = 20


def parse_options(args, valued, flags=()):
    """(options, positionals, problem) for `args`: each `valued` option takes the
    next argument, each flag stands alone, any other `--x` is a problem."""
    opts, pos, i = {}, [], 0
    while i < len(args):
        arg = args[i]
        if arg in valued:
            if i + 1 >= len(args):
                return None, None, "%s needs a value" % arg
            opts[arg] = args[i + 1]
            i += 2
        elif arg in flags:
            opts[arg] = True
            i += 1
        elif arg.startswith("--"):
            return None, None, "unknown option %r" % arg
        else:
            pos.append(arg)
            i += 1
    return opts, pos, None


def resume_text(root, rec):
    """The way back into one record's work: the host's native path first (omp
    `write agent://<id>` while the parent session lives, Claude `SendMessage`),
    else a re-dispatch brief that runs on any host or model - the original brief
    plus where the work stands (last commit, branch, uncommitted files)."""
    sid = rec["id"]
    lines = [tt.slice_line(rec)]
    if rec.get("kind") == "job":
        lines.append("  command: %s" % (rec.get("brief") or "(none recorded)"))
        return "\n".join(lines)
    tree = rec.get("worktree") or ""
    cwd = rec.get("cwd") or ""
    where = (tree if tree and os.path.isdir(tree) else
             cwd if cwd and os.path.isdir(cwd) else root)
    last = git_out(where, "log", "-1", "--format=%h %s") or "(none)"
    branch = (rec.get("branch") or git_out(where, "symbolic-ref", "-q", "--short", "HEAD")
              or "(detached HEAD)")
    # the journal's own directory is not the slice's work, ignored or not
    dirty = [line[3:] for line in (git_out(where, "status", "--porcelain") or "").splitlines()
             if not line[3:].startswith(".tezgah")]
    resume = ("Resume: last commit %s on %s; uncommitted: %s; continue from there, "
              "do not redo committed steps" % (last, branch, ", ".join(dirty) or "none"))
    # a Claude record named by a 12-hex brief digest addresses no agent: no
    # SendMessage line for it, only the re-dispatch
    named = not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{12}", sid)
    native = {"omp": "`write agent://%s` (while omp session %s still runs)"
                     % (sid, rec.get("parent") or "?"),
              "claude": "`SendMessage` to %s" % sid if named else None}.get(rec.get("host"))
    if native:
        lines.append("  native: %s with: %s" % (native, resume))
    provider = (" on another provider (`tezgah-route` names the tier; the same "
                "account's limit would stop it again)"
                if rec.get("failure") in ("quota", "rate_limit") else
                " on any host or model")
    lines.append("  %sre-dispatch%s with this brief:" % ("else " if native else "", provider))
    lines.append(rec.get("brief") or "(no brief recorded)")
    lines.append(resume)
    return "\n".join(lines)


def own_commits(root, rec):
    """Does the record's branch carry a commit past the base it was opened on?
    A branch still at its base is a slice that has not started, not one whose
    work landed."""
    base, branch = rec.get("base"), rec.get("branch")
    if not base or not branch:
        return False
    count = git_out(root, "rev-list", "--count", "%s..%s" % (base, branch))
    return bool(count) and count != "0"


def prune_branches(root, bases):
    """`git branch -d` every local branch whose work landed in `bases`, that no
    worktree has checked out, that tracks no upstream (a long-lived branch such
    as `develop` tracks one) and that carries a commit of its own (its reflog
    names one, the guard `_keeps` applies to a worktree: a pre-created branch
    nobody committed on is a slice not yet started) - never main, master or the
    current branch. One line per branch it deletes or git refuses."""
    current = git_out(root, "symbolic-ref", "-q", "--short", "HEAD")
    held = {line[len("branch refs/heads/"):] for line in
            (git_out(root, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain") or "").splitlines()
            if line.startswith("branch refs/heads/")}
    out = []
    for row in (git_out(root, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:short)\t%(upstream)",
                        "refs/heads") or "").splitlines():
        branch, _tab, upstream = row.partition("\t")
        if branch in ("main", "master", current) or branch in held or upstream:
            continue
        log = git_out(root, "reflog", "show", "--format=%gs", "refs/heads/" + branch) or ""
        if not any(entry.startswith(("commit:", "commit (amend):", "commit (initial):"))
                   for entry in log.splitlines()):
            continue
        if not any(_landed(root, branch, b) for b in bases):
            continue
        done = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "branch", "-d", branch],
                              capture_output=True, text=True)
        out.append("branch deleted: %s" % branch if done.returncode == 0 else
                   "branch kept: %s (`git branch -d %s` refused: %s)"
                   % (branch, branch, (done.stderr.strip().splitlines() or ["?"])[0]))
    return out


def _stamp(value):
    """Seconds since the epoch of an ISO-8601 UTC record time, None when bad."""
    import calendar
    try:
        return calendar.timegm(time.strptime(str(value), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"))
    except ValueError:
        return None


def slice_stats(rows, window, now=None):
    """Counts per status, failure class and host over the records updated in the
    last `window` days, and the candidate signatures: `class+host`, one event per
    record (the class it last stopped with) and `duplicate+host` per job record
    run more than once, at STATS_EVENTS events or STATS_SHARE of the slices."""
    from collections import Counter
    cutoff = (time.time() if now is None else now) - window * 86400
    picked = [r for r in rows if (_stamp(r.get("updated") or r.get("opened")) or 0) >= cutoff]
    sigs = Counter()
    for rec in picked:
        host = rec.get("host") or "?"
        if rec.get("failure") in LEVERS:
            sigs[(rec["failure"], host)] += 1
        if rec.get("kind") == "job" and int(rec.get("attempts") or 1) > 1:
            sigs[("duplicate", host)] += 1
    total = len(picked)
    candidates = [{"signature": "%s+%s" % key, "events": n,
                   "share": round(n / total, 3), "lever": LEVERS[key[0]]}
                  for key, n in sorted(sigs.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))
                  if n >= STATS_EVENTS
                  or (total >= STATS_SHARE_FLOOR and n >= STATS_SHARE * total)]
    return {"window_days": window, "slices": total,
            "status": dict(Counter(r.get("status") or "?" for r in picked)),
            "failure": dict(Counter(r["failure"] for r in picked if r.get("failure"))),
            "host": dict(Counter(r.get("host") or "?" for r in picked)),
            "candidates": candidates}


def _counts(counter):
    return ", ".join("%s %d" % kv for kv in sorted(counter.items())) or "none"


# Files every slice appends to and only the integrator resolves (research
# orchestrator-flow-audit S6, finding 8): a branch whose forecast conflict
# touches one merges after the others.
HOTSPOTS = ("MANIFEST", "docs/citations-baseline.json")


def merge_conflicts(root, ours, theirs):
    """The files `git merge-tree` forecasts in conflict when `theirs` merges
    into `ours`, minus the ones a `merge=union` attribute merges (CHANGELOG.md
    here); None when git cannot answer (no such ref, git before 2.38)."""
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "merge-tree", "--write-tree",
                              "--name-only", "--no-messages", ours, theirs],
                             capture_output=True, text=True)
    except OSError:
        return None
    if out.returncode not in (0, 1):
        return None
    files = sorted(set(out.stdout.split("\n\n")[0].splitlines()[1:]))
    if not files:
        return []
    attrs = git_out(root, "check-attr", "merge", "--", *files) or ""
    union = {line.rsplit(": merge: ", 1)[0] for line in attrs.splitlines()
             if line.endswith(": merge: union")}
    return [f for f in files if f not in union]


def merge_plan(root, base):
    """One line per done slice whose branch carries work `base` lacks, in the
    order to merge them: fewest forecast conflicts with `base` first, then a
    branch touching no HOTSPOTS, then fewest siblings it overlaps; each names
    its conflicting files and the siblings it collides with."""
    branches = {}
    for rec in tt.slices(root):
        branch = rec.get("branch")
        if (rec.get("status") == "done" and branch and branch not in branches
                and own_commits(root, rec) and not _landed(root, branch, base)):
            branches[branch] = rec
    rows = []
    for branch, rec in branches.items():
        mine = merge_conflicts(root, base, branch)
        if mine is None:
            continue
        overlaps = {}
        for other in branches:
            files = merge_conflicts(root, other, branch) if other != branch else None
            if files:
                overlaps[other] = files
        hot = any(f in HOTSPOTS for f in mine + sum(overlaps.values(), []))
        rows.append(((len(mine), hot, len(overlaps), branch), rec, mine, overlaps))
    rows.sort(key=lambda row: row[0])
    out = []
    for n, (key, rec, mine, overlaps) in enumerate(rows, 1):
        line = "%d. %s (slice %s): %d conflict(s) with %s%s" % (
            n, rec["branch"], rec["id"], len(mine), base,
            ": " + ", ".join(mine) if mine else "")
        for other, files in sorted(overlaps.items()):
            line += "; overlaps %s on %s" % (other, ", ".join(files))
        out.append(line + (" (a hotspot: the integrator resolves it)" if key[1] else ""))
    return out


def board_parent():
    """The parent whose slices `slice board` shows: the session in
    TEZGAH_SESSION, or - read from a subagent - the parent that spawned it
    (`tezgah_integrity.spawn_of`), so a slice sees its siblings; None, every
    open slice, outside a session."""
    session = os.environ.get("TEZGAH_SESSION")
    if not session:
        return None
    try:
        from tezgah_integrity import spawn_of
        spawn = spawn_of(session)
    except Exception:
        spawn = None
    return spawn[0] if spawn else session


def cmd_slice(args):
    usage = ("slice takes open|done|stop|reconcile|resume|stats|board|merge-plan "
             "(open --id ID [--kind agent|job] [--host H] [--agent A] "
             "[--brief TEXT|--brief-file F] [--parent P] [--cwd PATH]; done ID; stop ID "
             "[--class C]; reconcile [--prune]; resume ID; stats [--window DAYS] "
             "[--json]; board [--parent P]; merge-plan [--base REF])")
    if not args:
        return misuse(usage)
    sub, rest = args[0], args[1:]
    root = repo_root(os.getcwd())
    if sub == "board":
        opts, pos, bad = parse_options(rest, ("--parent",))
        if bad or pos:
            return misuse(bad or "slice board takes [--parent P] and nothing else")
        lines = tt.board(root, opts.get("--parent") or board_parent())
        print("\n".join(lines) if lines else "no open slice")
        return 0
    if sub == "merge-plan":
        opts, pos, bad = parse_options(rest, ("--base",))
        if bad or pos:
            return misuse(bad or "slice merge-plan takes [--base REF] and nothing else")
        base = opts.get("--base", "HEAD")
        if git_out(root, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", base) is None:
            return problems(["no revision %s in %s" % (base, root)])
        lines = merge_plan(root, base)
        print("\n".join(lines) if lines else "no done slice branch to merge into %s" % base)
        return 0
    if sub == "open":
        opts, pos, bad = parse_options(rest, ("--id", "--kind", "--host", "--agent",
                                              "--brief", "--brief-file", "--parent",
                                              "--cwd"))
        if bad or pos:
            return misuse(bad or "slice open takes options only (%r)" % pos[0])
        if not opts.get("--id"):
            return misuse("slice open needs --id ID")
        if "--brief" in opts and "--brief-file" in opts:
            return misuse("--brief and --brief-file contradict each other")
        kind, host = opts.get("--kind", "agent"), opts.get("--host", "")
        if kind not in tt.SLICE_KINDS:
            return misuse("--kind is one of %s" % ", ".join(tt.SLICE_KINDS))
        if host and host not in tt.SLICE_HOSTS:
            return misuse("--host is one of %s" % ", ".join(tt.SLICE_HOSTS))
        brief = opts.get("--brief", "")
        if "--brief-file" in opts:
            try:
                brief = read(opts["--brief-file"])
            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:
                return problems(["cannot read %s: %s" % (opts["--brief-file"], exc)])
        try:
            rec = tt.open_slice(root, opts["--id"], kind=kind, host=host,
                                agent=opts.get("--agent", ""), brief=brief,
                                parent=opts.get("--parent", ""),
                                cwd=opts.get("--cwd") or os.getcwd())
        except OSError as exc:
            return problems(["cannot write the slice record: %s" % exc])
        print("slice %s open (%s, attempt %d)%s" % (
            rec["id"], rec["kind"], rec["attempts"],
            " worktree %s on %s" % (rec["worktree"], rec["branch"] or "?")
            if rec["worktree"] else ""))
        return 0
    if sub in ("done", "stop"):
        opts, pos, bad = parse_options(rest, ("--class",) if sub == "stop" else ())
        if bad or len(pos) != 1:
            return misuse(bad or "slice %s takes one ID%s" % (
                sub, " [--class C]" if sub == "stop" else ""))
        failure = opts.get("--class", "")
        if failure and failure not in tt.FAILURES:
            return misuse("--class is one of %s" % ", ".join(tt.FAILURES))
        status = "done" if sub == "done" else "stopped"
        try:
            found = tt.find_slice(root, pos[0], os.environ.get("TEZGAH_SESSION"))
            rec = found and tt.close_slice(root, found["id"], status, failure)
        except OSError as exc:
            return problems(["cannot write the slice record: %s" % exc])
        if not rec:
            return problems(["no slice record %s under %s" % (pos[0], tt.slice_dir(root))])
        print("slice %s %s%s" % (rec["id"], status,
                                 " (%s)" % rec["failure"] if rec.get("failure") else ""))
        return 0
    if sub == "reconcile":
        opts, pos, bad = parse_options(rest, (), ("--prune",))
        if bad or pos:
            return misuse(bad or "slice reconcile takes --prune and nothing else")
        if opts.get("--prune"):
            bases = landing_bases(root)
            # read before the prune: a removed worktree takes its branch with it
            landed = [rec for rec in tt.slices(root)
                      if rec.get("status") != "done" and own_commits(root, rec)
                      and any(_landed(root, rec["branch"], b) for b in bases)]
            for line in prune_worktrees(root) + prune_branches(root, bases):
                print(line)
            for rec in landed:
                tt.close_slice(root, rec["id"], "done")
                print("slice %s done: %s landed" % (rec["id"], rec["branch"]))
        rows = tt.pending(root)
        if not rows:
            print("no open or stopped slice")
        for rec in rows:
            print(resume_text(root, rec))
        return 0
    if sub == "resume":
        if len(rest) != 1:
            return misuse("slice resume takes one ID")
        rec = tt.find_slice(root, rest[0], os.environ.get("TEZGAH_SESSION"))
        if rec is None:
            return problems(["no slice record %s under %s" % (rest[0], tt.slice_dir(root))])
        print(resume_text(root, rec))
        tt.close_slice(root, rec["id"], "resumed")
        return 0
    if sub == "stats":
        opts, pos, bad = parse_options(rest, ("--window",), ("--json",))
        if bad or pos:
            return misuse(bad or "slice stats takes [--window DAYS] [--json]")
        window = opts.get("--window", str(STATS_WINDOW))
        if not window.isdigit() or int(window) < 1:
            return misuse("--window takes a whole number of days")
        stats = slice_stats(tt.slices(root), int(window))
        if opts.get("--json"):
            print(json.dumps(stats, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
            return 0
        print("slices in the last %d days: %d" % (stats["window_days"], stats["slices"]))
        for key in ("status", "failure", "host"):
            print("  %s: %s" % (key, _counts(stats[key])))
        for row in stats["candidates"]:
            print("candidate: %s - %d events (%d%% of slices) - lever: %s" % (
                row["signature"], row["events"], round(row["share"] * 100), row["lever"]))
        if not stats["candidates"]:
            print("no signature at %d events or %d%% of the slices"
                  % (STATS_EVENTS, round(STATS_SHARE * 100)))
        return 0
    return misuse(usage)


def main(argv):
    if len(argv) < 2:
        print(__doc__, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    cmd, rest = argv[1], argv[2:]
    if cmd == "start":
        return cmd_start(rest)
    if cmd == "phase":
        return cmd_phase(rest)
    if cmd == "allow":
        return cmd_allow(rest)
    if cmd == "stop":
        return cmd_stop(rest)
    if cmd == "status":
        return cmd_status(rest)
    if cmd == "review":
        return cmd_review(rest)
    if cmd == "close":
        return cmd_close(rest)
    if cmd == "tick":
        return cmd_tick(rest)
    if cmd == "note":
        return cmd_note(rest)
    if cmd == "slice":
        return cmd_slice(rest)
    return misuse("unknown command %r (start, phase, allow, stop, status, review, "
                  "close, tick, note, slice)" % cmd)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
